 21. Testing each issue

| Issue | How to test |
|---|---|
| Alt text | WAVE, or turn off images and read the alt text |
| Contrast | Colour Contrast Analyser, Lighthouse |
| Keyboard / focus | Unplug the mouse; use Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter, Esc |
| Captions / transcripts | Play the video with sound off |
| Text size | Zoom to 200% and check nothing is lost |
| Links / headings | Screen reader links list and headings list (NVDA, TalkBack) |
| Forms | Submit empty and wrong data; check error messages |
| Colour alone | View in greyscale or a colour-blindness simulator |
| Flashing | Watch animations; use the PEAT tool |
| Time limits | Leave a session idle and see what happens |
| Reflow | Resize the browser to 320 px or use mobile emulation |

## 22. Why accessibility matters
- **Ethical:** everyone has an equal right to information and services.
- **Legal:** many countries have disability and equality laws, including Uganda's Persons with Disabilities Act (2020).
- **Practical:** captions help in noisy places, good contrast helps in sunlight, and plain language helps everyone.
- **Business:** wider audience, better SEO and fewer abandoned forms.

## 23. Conclusion (sample wording)
"The audit identified fifteen accessibility barriers affecting people with visual, hearing, motor and cognitive disabilities. The most serious are keyboard inaccessibility, unlabelled forms, time limits and flashing content, because they block users completely or risk harm. Applying the recommended improvements would make the websites more inclusive, easier to use and compliant with WCAG 2.1 Level AA."

24. Likely exam questions
1. **Why do images need alt text?** Screen readers read it aloud so blind users understand the image.
2. **What is the difference between captions and a transcript?** Captions are synced with the video; a transcript is a separate full text version.
3. **Why is "click here" a poor link text?** Screen readers list links out of context, so it gives no clue about the destination.
4. **Why should colour not be the only way to show information?** Colour-blind and low-vision users may not see the difference.
5. **What is a keyboard trap?** A place where keyboard focus gets stuck and cannot leave.
6. **How many flashes per second are considered dangerous?** More than three.
7. **Why are time limits a barrier?** Users who work slowly are locked out before finishing.
8. **What is responsive design?** A layout that adapts to any screen size or zoom level.

 25. Key terms
- WCAG: Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, the international standard.
- Alt text: a text alternative describing an image.
- Screen reader: software that reads screen content aloud (NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack).
- Captions: synced text for the audio in a video.
- Transcript: a full text version of audio content.
- Focus indicator: the visible outline on the active element.
- **Reflow:** content adjusting to the screen without horizontal scrolling.
- **Plain language:** clear, simple wording that most people can understand.

