33. More likely exam questions
1. What is a skip link? A link at the top of the page that lets keyboard users jump past the menu to the main content.
2. Why are image-only PDFs inaccessible? Screen readers cannot read the text, and it cannot be resized.
3. Why should tables have header cells? Screen readers use them to tell users which column and row each value belongs to.
4. What does WCAG 2.5.8 require? Interactive targets of at least 24×24 px.
5. Give two ways to make a CAPTCHA more accessible. Provide an audio alternative, or use an invisible or logic-based check.
6. Why must menus be consistent across pages? Predictable layout helps people with cognitive disabilities and everyone else.
7. What is the "first rule of ARIA"? Use native HTML elements before adding ARIA.
8. Why is automated testing not enough? Tools find only about 30 to 40% of issues and cannot judge meaning, such as whether alt text is accurate.

 34. More key terms
- Bypass blocks: a way to skip repeated content such as menus.
- Tagged PDF: a PDF with structure (headings, lists) that assistive technology can read.
- Modal: a pop-up window that blocks the rest of the page until closed.
- Target size: the clickable or tappable area of a control.
- Redundant entry: asking users to enter the same information twice.
- Accessibility statement: a page describing a site's accessibility level and how to report problems.

